<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">French Flanders</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Flandre française</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">French <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/flanders">Flanders</a> is the part of the historical County of Flanders that belongs to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, in today's Nord <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/departement">department</a>, around <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lille">Lille</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/douai">Douai</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dunkirk">Dunkirk</a> and Hazebrouck. It was won by France in the seventeenth century: Dunkirk was bought from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> in 1662, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-xiv">Louis XIV</a>'s conquests were confirmed by the treaties of Aix-la-Chapelle in 1668 and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nijmegen">Nijmegen</a> in 1678. Its northern district kept a Flemish-speaking tradition.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/french-flanders.jpg" alt="French Flanders" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: SEPB66 · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_French-Flanders.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
